#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128797 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0a99e46a-2104-5285-862b-96b98e7a2f85" :question/math-academy-id "e-12482" :question/problem "A triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines $y = x$ and $x = 1$, and the $x$-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/40f38ffcf79abe1b11cb7e59b6dd1efb.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n$I_x = \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n$I_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n$I_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/60a078ad0268df77d16370adbb8a2cfa.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = k \\mid y \\mid = k y\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= ∬_(D)λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}kydydx \\\\\n&= k∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{x}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= k∫_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{2}y^{2}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= k∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2}∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{k}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k = 6M$ and hence\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = ky = 6M y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{y} &= ∬_(D)x^{2}λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= 6M∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}x^{2} \\cdot ydydx \\\\\n&= 6M∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{x}x^{2}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= 6M∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}[\\frac{1}{2}y^{2}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= 6M∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= 3M[\\frac{1}{5}x^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3M}{5}[x^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3M}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128798 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "dbf6a3b7-abb9-5f62-83af-00169c673bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12483" :question/problem "Consider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r = 1$ and $r = 2$ and the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}$. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/fe53edf914e85df412be64f421b172c0.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(r,\\theta)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n$I_x = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\sin^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta$.\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n$I_y = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\cos^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta$.\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n$I_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta = I_x + I_y$.\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{y} &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ) \\cdot r^{3}\\cos^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/4}∫_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ} \\cdot r^{3}\\cos^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/4}∫_{1}^{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= (∫_{1}^{2}rdr)(∫_{π/6}^{π/4}dθ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}r^{2} \\mid _{1}^{2} \\cdot θ \\mid _{π/6}^{π/4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4 - 1) \\cdot (\\frac{π}{4} - \\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\frac{π}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128965 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/id #uuid "c317696a-899d-5af5-9bf4-2d38862f5877" :question/math-academy-id "e-8542" :question/problem "A triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=2-x$, as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/ae2c087be0a73aeee4dc4137c925a39b.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n$I_x = \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n$I_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n$I_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2-x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/1ca849c74b98783c11e88da02f294af8.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 2\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{M}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{x} &= ∬_(D)y^{2}λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{2}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{2 - x}y^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{2}∫_{0}^{2}[∫_{0}^{2 - x}y^{2}dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{2}∫_{0}^{2}[\\frac{y^{3}}{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2 - x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{6}∫_{0}^{2}(2 - x)^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{6}[- \\frac{1}{4}(2 - x)^{4}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{M}{24}[(2 - x)^{4}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{M}{24}(0^{4} - 2^{4}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{M}{24} \\cdot (-16) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2M}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128966 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bee275c6-cca6-515f-b737-597ecca278a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8543" :question/problem "A plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 4xy$, find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/72446ce285072cf28a0dcd840a3d6893.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n$I_x = \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n$I_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A$.\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n$I_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y$.\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/085cf7b65bcf36333d18ac045baf3641.png)\n\nUsing the formula for $I_x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{x} &= ∬_(D)y^{2}λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{x^{2}}^{1}y^{2} \\cdot 4xydydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{x^{2}}^{1}4xy^{3}dy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[xy^{4}]_{y = x^{2}}^{y = 1}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x(1 - x^{8})dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x - x^{9}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - \\frac{x^{10}}{10}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9126cc15-7844-5e66-9f70-dab2c2160171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126603} {:answer/id #uuid "c04c0f2e-0383-5017-88c7-88f3d6cdd2de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126604} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3c3851-ba2c-525c-af96-153abdbffc36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126605} {:answer/id #uuid "21e05994-2fd0-59df-b36a-ab1bd8173803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2Mkg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126606} {:answer/id #uuid "35d23242-7b78-5f55-912a-9b1f259295dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d23242-7b78-5f55-912a-9b1f259295dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{3}kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126607} :answer-field/id #uuid "461d0f88-bdc4-5802-bb80-da9393ed9cfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126602}] :question/id #uuid "d5a597a5-f786-5891-91b3-c78b73413f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-162905" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $Mkg$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 2}$, where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89ba08b8-91dd-5d58-ae3e-df468b5dc5cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126615} {:answer/id #uuid "9883c1c9-1ba7-5513-935e-9156ca991880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126616} {:answer/id #uuid "c87d31f2-badd-5487-a34b-69fb837e6098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126617} {:answer/id #uuid "e04e8d3a-2d3e-5670-84d2-05f520922e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126618} {:answer/id #uuid "7f801006-1ed9-5f95-938c-371a7fc6a3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e04e8d3a-2d3e-5670-84d2-05f520922e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97.5kg \\cdot m^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126618} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e94759f-a518-58ea-85ee-780e8566772b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126614}] :question/id #uuid "e40dca43-d21c-5ed7-bbc5-5f37b500695b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162922" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$\nwith mass density function $λ(x, y) = (6 - xy)kg/m^{2}$, where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "889e1bde-98ec-5520-81d3-bfc61b369742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126627} {:answer/id #uuid "90131c7e-7376-577f-baa2-1714df60568d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126628} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2842e7-e4e9-5230-b246-d1bd1565af15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126629} {:answer/id #uuid "13e80e32-a0e9-5331-a0bb-e614d0752b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126630} {:answer/id #uuid "342c2a96-e444-508c-aa2c-b9df291a1df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "889e1bde-98ec-5520-81d3-bfc61b369742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126627} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8c9c55-6640-5a69-8e00-97608a2660ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126626}] :question/id #uuid "54c0ec35-7150-5d58-acd5-421129a7e7ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-162986" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$.\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6d95c32-d6b0-52f0-9e18-ded300260902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126633} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe2fd3e-425d-5278-a8af-ccfe6d086112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126634} {:answer/id #uuid "f188ff5e-3ac1-535a-9ca9-d8daf20bc68d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126635} {:answer/id #uuid "2d031545-04fb-56a1-94d8-4f128a3345dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126636} {:answer/id #uuid "7b230073-2983-5000-a28d-28363f80c07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d031545-04fb-56a1-94d8-4f128a3345dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126636} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7e6a23-873d-56aa-b92e-49a46d29281e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126632}] :question/id #uuid "0584f4c5-c47d-5f81-8ff4-5bc6e28a681f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162987" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2027/Source/Images/q-162987.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the line $y = 2 - x$ and the coordinate axes. The mass density at each point is proportional to the sum of its distance from the $x$-axis and its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis."}]}
